re T2, 4 left).  \n- B1, B2, B5: isolated; B1 and B2 could form chow B1-B2-B3 (B3: 3 left), but B5 is alone.  \n\nBest discard is likely one that doesn't contribute to potential melds and is safe. B5 is a middle tile (5) and there are 4 left, but it's not connecting well with my other tiles (B1 and B2 are 1 and 2, so B5 is distant). B5 could be part of chows like B4-B5-B6 or B5-B6-B7, but I have no B4 or B6. So B5 is least useful. Also, B5 has 4 left, so discarding it is not too dangerous (others may not need it urgently). Alternatively, discarding B1 or B2 might break a potential chow, but I only have one of each, so keeping them doesn't help much unless I draw exactly B3.  \n\nComparing:  \n- Play B5: discards an isolated middle tile with 4 left, relatively safe.  \n- Play B1 or B2: discards a terminal (B1) or 2 (B2), but they might be needed for chow if I draw B3. But probability low.  \n- Play T1 or T2: I have T1 and T2, could form chow with T3, but I have only one T1 and two T2; discarding T1 loses that possibility, discarding T2 reduces chance for pung/chow.  \n- Play W1 or W2: I have multiple W1 and W2, so discarding one might be okay, but they are more useful for potential W flush or pungs.  \n\nGiven my exposed meld is in W suit, sticking to W suit might be good for half flush or full flush possibilities. So I should keep W tiles. B5 is the clearest discard.\n\nReason: B5 is an isolated middle tile with no strong connections to my other tiles, and there are 4 copies left so it's relatively safe to discard. Keeping W tiles supports potential flush patterns given my existing W chow. Discarding B5 minimizes loss of potential melds.\n\nAnswer: Play B5"}
